---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../components/SectionHeading.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import { profile } from '../data/site';
import { url } from '../lib/url';
---
<Base
  title="About"
  description="How Tebit Ramson Titih went from a first CRUD form to building a school management platform and a Laravel media pipeline — and what he is trying to get good at next."
>
  <article class="section">
    <div class="page">
      <SectionHeading
        index="01"
        eyebrow="About"
        title="I got here by building the wrong thing first."
        as="h1"
        lead="A short, honest account of where I am, how I work, and what I am aiming at."
      />

      <div class="prose story">
        <p data-reveal>
          I am an HND Software Engineering student at <strong>Saint Louis University
          Institute in Douala</strong>. That is the formal version. The useful version is
          that I started writing PHP because I wanted to see a form I had typed put a
          row into a database, and I have not really stopped since.
        </p>

        <p data-reveal>
          The first thing I finished was a student registration system — a form, a
          table, search, sorting, pagination, a CSV export. It worked. It was also,
          I now know, full of the exact mistakes every beginner makes: SQL built by
          string concatenation, database credentials committed to the repository,
          and a delete route that anyone who could guess a URL could fire. I have
          kept it public. It is the clearest evidence I have that I learn.
        </p>

        <h2 data-reveal>The problem I keep coming back to</h2>

        <p data-reveal>
          Cameroonian secondary schools grade on a system that imported software
          consistently gets wrong. Marks out of twenty. Three terms of two sequences
          each. And every subject carrying its own ministry-set coefficient, so a
          student's average is a weighted mean, not a plain one. Software built
          around a 0–100 scale does not merely look foreign here — it produces
          incorrect report cards.
        </p>

        <p data-reveal>
          So most of that work is still done by hand, with a calculator and a stack
          of mark sheets, for days at a time every term. My School Management System
          is my attempt at the version that understands the local model properly.
          It is the project I care most about, and the one that has taught me the
          most about what happens when your data model does not match reality.
        </p>

        <h2 data-reveal>How I work</h2>

        <p data-reveal>
          <strong>I start with the schema.</strong> Before any controller exists I
          want to know what the tables are, what is unique, what cascades, and which
          invariants the database should enforce rather than trust me to remember.
          Most of the bugs I have had to fix late were decisions I made too quickly
          early.
        </p>

        <p data-reveal>
          <strong>I audit my own code.</strong> Partway through the school system I
          stopped adding features and reviewed everything as though someone else had
          written it. I found duplicated averaging logic in three places, a ranking
          computed from a different number than the one displayed, and file names
          whose casing would break the moment the project met a Linux server. That
          list is published in the repository. Writing it down was uncomfortable and
          it is the only reason those things got fixed.
        </p>

        <p data-reveal>
          <strong>I prefer the simple tool.</strong> The school system deliberately
          uses no framework, because its real deployment target is a school with
          shared Apache hosting and no developer on staff — there, a build step is a
          failure mode. The video studio uses Laravel, because queues, migrations and
          policies were genuinely the problem. The stack should follow the constraint,
          not the fashion.
        </p>

        <p data-reveal>
          <strong>I use AI as a reviewer, not an author.</strong> It is very good at
          finding the thing I missed at one in the morning and very bad at knowing
          what a Cameroonian report card has to contain. I read everything before it
          lands.
        </p>

        <h2 data-reveal>Where I am going</h2>

        <p data-reveal>
          I want to work somewhere my code is reviewed by people better than me. I
          am looking for an internship or a junior backend role — locally or
          remotely, and I am open to opportunities outside Cameroon. What I bring
          right now is not seniority: it is that I finish things, I go back and fix
          what I got wrong, and I can hold a non-trivial domain in my head long
          enough to model it properly.
        </p>

        <p data-reveal>
          Next on the list: getting genuinely fluent with automated testing beyond
          what I have already written, and learning to deploy and operate something
          on real infrastructure rather than on XAMPP.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="about__cta" data-reveal>
        <a class="button button--primary" href={url('/projects')}>
          Read the case studies
          <Icon name="arrow" size={16} />
        </a>
        <a class="button button--secondary" href={url('/resume')}>Experience &amp; education</a>
        <a class="button button--secondary" href={`mailto:${profile.email}`}>Email me</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .story { --flow: var(--space-m); }
  .story > * + * { margin-top: var(--flow); }
  .story p { color: var(--text-muted); }
  .story strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 580; }

  .story h2 {
    margin-top: var(--space-xl);
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    padding-top: var(--space-s);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .about__cta {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
    margin-top: var(--space-2xl);
    padding-top: var(--space-l);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
</style>
